posts.body:
@ATT_Turan
you have a good point. Unfortunately, MZ is not available for Linux yet, I can't afford to purchase Windows and there's not enough reviews on ProtonDB (besides one negative) to encourage me to try it.
I agree with you that a "Learn RPG Maker MZ" certainly would be a much better shot for me, although many of what one can learn through my lessons can also be used with it (and even with other engines).

@ATT_Turan
you may not believe me (or think I'm an ignorant) but I have never heard of James Newton Howard before. I had to Google his name to learn he composed Batman Begins OST. However, I understand your point and I probably would take lessons from Marcus Vianna instead of someone I never heard of and with no references on this context.
@SigmaSuccour
, thanks again for your comments. Your point is clear for me now and I managed to understand many things through your honest feedback.
I would like to ask a question for you: "A demo of this game with a few lessons would increase your interest in checking/supporting it?"

@CleanWater
looking at your screenshots and the, well, language your Steam releases are in, it looks like you could use some help with English-language localization. (I could help with this if you have a budget for it I don't know how well your crowdfunding is going.)
Everyone else has already said anything I would in general about the way this is being framed/marketed, so I won't repeat their points. At a glance it looks like you're taking constructive criticism on those things like a champ.

I've been silently following the progression of things here, and to
@CleanWater
and
@SigmaSuccour
, I have to give you guys major props. The interaction between you two is the sort of thing I really wish I saw more of online. The way neither of you just outright dismiss each other's words and instead calmly respond to the other is absolutely wonderful. Keep it up you two.

The last lesson, Battles, is now available!
You can check more details about this update on this official announcement.
